# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Compiled Go binaries (built by `go build`)
backend/api-gateway
backend/parsing-worker
backend/ingestion-watcher
backend/cve-refresher

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts
*.out
coverage.*
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# env file
.env

# Local kind deployment (secrets, overrides)
local/

# Editor/IDE
.idea/
.vscode/

# Hugo documentation site (build artifacts)
docs/public/
docs/resources/
docs/node_modules/
__pycache__/

# Local scratch space: release-note drafts, one-off patch scripts, SBOMs
# pulled in for manual debugging. Named consistently so a stray file can
# never be swept into a commit by `git add -A`.
tmp-*
patch_api.py

# Demo SBOMs, kept out of vulnerability scanners — not out of git.
#
# examples/ carries sample SBOMs with deliberately outdated packages so a fresh
# install has findings to show and VEX statements to apply. They are sample
# *data*: nothing in examples/ is built, imported or shipped. osv-scanner cannot
# tell that apart from a manifest and reported all of it as if BOMHort depended
# on it — 79 of the 82 vulnerabilities in the OpenSSF Scorecard report came from
# here, which is what pinned that check at 0.
#
# osv-scanner honours .gitignore (that is what `--no-ignore` turns off), and
# these files are already tracked, so ignoring them changes nothing about what
# is in the repository — only what scanners walk into.
#
# Consequence: a NEW demo SBOM needs `git add -f`. That is the trade for not
# having to rename the files away from the .spdx.json / .cdx.json convention
# this project itself documents.
examples/**/*.spdx.json
examples/**/*.cdx.json
